          %meta{:content => "application/xhtml+xml;charset=utf-8", "http-equiv" => "Content-type"}/
          %title Too simple to be simple
          %h1#too_simple_to_be_simple Too simple to be simple
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#relationship_to_biased_definitions"} Relationship to biased definitions
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p There is a general principle in mathematics that
          .standout
            %p A trivial object is too simple to be simple.
          %p
            Quite often, classical references will define ‘simple’ (or an analogous term) in naïve way, so that a ‘trivial’ object
            %em is
            simple, but later it will become clear that more sophisticated theorems (especially classification theorems) work better if the definition is changed so that the trivial object is
            %em not
            simple. Usually this can be done by changing ‘if’ to ‘iff’ (or sometimes changing ‘or’ to ‘xor’) in the classical definition.
          %p The general pattern is a progression of definitions (of ‘simple’) from more to less naïve:
          %ol
            %li Imprecise summary: there are no foos. (Example: in a field, every element is invertible.)
            %li
              Classical definition: there are no nontrivial foos. (Example: in a field, every element other than
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is invertible.)
            %li
              Modern definition: there are no nontrivial foos, but there is the trivial foo. (Example: in a field, every element other than
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is invertible, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              is non-invertible.)
          %p
            This should be distinguished from barring the trivial object entirely. Historically, many people wanted to say that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            is not a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+ring"} trivial ring
            is not a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            or that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/improper+filter"} improper filter
            is not a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filter"} filter
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter"} ultrafilter
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p Examples include:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one"} 1
              is not a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+number"} prime number
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+ring"} trivial ring
              is not a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral domain
              (although note that the notions of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibly+trivial+integral+domain"} possibly trivial integral domain
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibly+trivial+field"} possibly trivial field
              also exist).
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+group"} trivial group
              is not a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+group"} simple group
              (which is the trope-namer).
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+object"} zero object
              is not a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+object"} simple object
              (generalizing the previous example).
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty space
              is not a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path-connected+space"} path-connected space
              or even a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected space
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/improper+ideal"} improper ideal
              is not a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+ideal"} maximal ideal
              (or even a
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+ideal"} prime ideal
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/improper+filter"} improper filter
              is not an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ultrafilter"} ultrafilter
            %li
              An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+function"} empty function
              is not a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constant+function"} constant function
            %li
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bottom+element"} bottom element
              is not an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atom"} atom
          %p
            But of course one may still find definitions used that disagree (see discussion at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected space
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibly+trivial+field"} possibly trivial field
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibly+trivial+integral+domain"} possibly trivial integral domain
            for how to modify these to allow the trivial ring constructively).
          %p Perhaps the basic example is that
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} False
              is not
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
          %p
            Nobody would be naïve enough to believe otherwise in this case, of course. The point with this example is that, in the other cases, the naïve definition imposes only a uniqueness requirement (so that some set of possibilities —such as the set of proper divisors of a prime number, or the set of non-invertible elements of a field— must be a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingleton"} subsingleton
            %em existence and uniqueness
            requirement (so that the set of possibilities must be a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singleton"} singleton
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+values"} truth values
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-1%29-truncation"} truncation
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-2)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28-2%29-truncation"} truncation
          %h2#relationship_to_biased_definitions Relationship to biased definitions
          %p
            In many of the above examples one can obtain the sophisticated definition from the naïve definition by replacing a
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is prime if whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = a b
              we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = a
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = b
          %p with
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              is prime if whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \prod_{i=1}^k a_i
              we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = a_i
              for some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %p
            Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is not a prime because it is equal to the empty product (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = 0
            ) but not equal to any of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
            (because there aren’t any)! Similarly we have:
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+space"} empty space
              is the empty union (of
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/clopen+subspaces"} clopen subspaces
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected
            %li
              In the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+ring"} trivial ring
              the empty product is equal to zero, and so this ring is not
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/false"} False
              is the empty disjunction, and hence is not
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
          %p
            This illustrates one advantage of using unbiased rather than
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biased+definition"} biased
            definitions: if one has replaced “for all
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            and
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            case and end up with a definition that fails for the trivial case. (Unfortunately, it seems to be easy to forget
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            even when using unbiased definitions, but hopefully less easy.)
          %p
            In a similar vein we can define
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path-connected+space"} path connected
            by
          %ul
            %li
              A space is path connected if for each (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kuratowski-finite"}> Kuratowski
              )-finite subset there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path"} path
              (a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
              from the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+interval"} unit interval
          %p Then the empty space is not path connected because it has no paths at all and hence no path through the empty subset.
